course: dictionary
lesson: 11163
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word window can mean a glass opening in a wall, a part of a computer screen, a shop front, or a short time. Context tells you which meaning is right. «If you lose your key, do not climb in through a window; call a parent or go to a trusted neighbour.» You climb through an opening in a wall, so here a window is the glass opening in a house.
explain: Read the words around the word. «A boy in Leeds broke a neighbour's window with a ball and hid.» Broke tells you the window is made of glass. Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Other words in the sentence can point to a meaning too. «Look out of the window at the far trees» tells you a window lets you see outside. «Keeping heat in with thick walls, roof insulation and closed windows saves more than anything else» tells you a window can be shut to keep warm air in.
fact: A window is an opening in a wall, usually with glass, to let in light and air.
fact: On a computer, a window is one part of the screen where a program shows.
fact: A shop window shows goods to people outside, and window shopping means looking, not buying.
fact: A window of time is a short time when something can be done.
exercise: «If you lose your key, do not climb in through a window; call a parent or go to a trusted neighbour.» What is a window in this sentence? | A shop front with goods to see | *An opening in a wall, with glass, to let in light and air | A short time when something can be done | A part of a computer screen
exercise: «I have a window before tea to finish my homework.» What does window mean here? | A part of a computer screen | Glass in a shop front | *A short time when something can be done | An opening in a wall
exercise: «A shop window showed hats and bags to people outside.» What did the shop window do? | Opened for fresh air | *Showed goods to people outside | Ran a program | Let in light for plants
test: «Look out of the window at the far trees.» What is a window in this sentence? | *An opening in a wall, with glass | A part of a computer screen | A short time to act | A shop display of hats
test: Which sentence uses window to mean a part of a computer screen? | He had a window to call his gran before lunch. | *The game ran in its own window on the screen. | She opened the window to let in some fresh air. | We went window shopping on Saturday.
exam: «Keeping heat in with thick walls, roof insulation and closed windows saves more than anything else.» What do closed windows help to do here? | Show goods to people outside | Let in more air | Run a computer program | *Keep heat in
